[["HTTP","The Protocol That Carries the Web","IT 3203 Assignment 1: Research and Learn Web Evolution\nPrepared by Nasir Hassan • Fall 2026","Research focus\nOne web component: Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP). The deck explains its purpose, history, current status, and future direction.","Browser  ⇄  HTTP  ⇄  Web Server","Requests, responses, headers, status codes, caching, security, APIs, performance","Sources: Assignment prompt; MDN HTTP; IETF RFC 9110–9114"],["Overview","IT 3203 • Research and Learn Web Evolution • 02","Executive overview: what this report teaches","HTTP is the common language that lets browsers, servers, apps, APIs, and CDNs exchange web resources.","Main idea\nHTTP began as a very small protocol for asking a server for one hypertext document. It became a flexible application protocol for documents, media, APIs, streaming, security policies, and high-performance web applications.","Why it matters\nEvery time a user loads a web page, submits a form, opens a web app, streams media, or calls a REST API, HTTP is usually involved. The protocol affects speed, reliability, privacy, and security.","Evolution pattern\nHTTP did not evolve by replacing the web. It kept the same basic request/response semantics while improving the way messages move across networks: HTTP/1.1 improved reuse, HTTP/2 improved multiplexing, and HTTP/3 moved to QUIC.","Current status\nAs of September 2026, W3Techs reports HTTP/2 on 34.8% of websites and HTTP/3 on 40.7%. HTTP/1.1 remains important because older servers, clients, networks, and fallback paths still exist.","Future direction\nThe future of HTTP is connected to encrypted-by-default traffic, QUIC-based delivery, privacy-preserving protocol design, more efficient prioritization, edge computing, and web transports for real-time applications.","Sources: RFC 9110; RFC 9113; RFC 9114; W3Techs HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 surveys, September 2026"],["Overview","IT 3203 • Research and Learn Web Evolution • 03","Research scope and learning goals","This report focuses on one technical component of the web: HTTP, not browsers, HTML, URLs, or web servers.","Research question\nHow did HTTP evolve from a simple document-transfer protocol into the modern system that supports secure, high-performance web applications?","Identify HTTP’s role in the World Wide Web and explain why browsers and servers need it.\n\nTrace HTTP from the early World Wide Web at CERN through HTTP/0.9, HTTP/1.0, HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3.\n\nExplain core concepts: clients, servers, requests, responses, headers, methods, status codes, caching, cookies, and TLS/HTTPS.\n\nCompare the technical design goals of each major HTTP generation: simplicity, extensibility, performance, security, and reliability.\n\nEvaluate recent developments and future directions including QUIC, HTTP/3 adoption, prioritization, privacy, APIs, and edge platforms.","Audience design\nThe slides use plain explanations, examples, diagrams, and analogies so a high school student can learn without hearing a live presentation. The report uses detailed slide text instead of short speaker-only bullet points.","Deliverable check\nThe assignment asks for a slide-based research report with at least 20 main-content slides, one web component, and at least 7 references. This deck includes more than 20 content slides and a reference list.","Sources: Assignment prompt lines 9–24, 33–39"],["Core Concepts","IT 3203 • Research and Learn Web Evolution • 04","Where HTTP fits in a web request","HTTP is the application-layer conversation that happens after network addressing and transport are ready.","1. Browser\nThe client starts the request when the user types a URL, clicks a link, submits a form, or a script calls fetch().","2. DNS/IP/TLS\nBefore HTTP content moves, the computer usually resolves a domain name, opens a connection, and negotiates encryption.","3. HTTP\nThe client sends a request message. The server returns a response message with a status, headers, and optional body.","4. Web server/CDN\nThe server or CDN locates the resource, applies rules such as caching or authentication, and sends data back.","HTTP does not decide how a web page looks. HTML describes page structure, CSS controls appearance, and JavaScript controls behavior. HTTP is the delivery and coordination protocol that gets those files and API responses from server to client.","RFC 9110 defines HTTP as a stateless, application-level, request/response protocol with shared semantics across different versions. That means the meaning of “GET,” “404,” or “Content-Type” remains consistent even when the transport changes.","Sources: RFC 9110; MDN Evolution of HTTP"],["Core Concepts","IT 3203 • Research and Learn Web Evolution • 05","The request/response pattern: HTTP as a conversation","A browser asks for something; a server answers with a result or an error.","HTTP request\nExample: GET /products.html HTTP/1.1\nHost: example.com\nAccept: text/html","HTTP response\nExample: HTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: text/html\nCache-Control: max-age=3600","request goes out","response comes back","A response has a status code\n200 means success. 301/302 mean redirect. 404 means not found. 500 means the server failed. These codes let browsers and apps react automatically instead of guessing.","Headers carry metadata\nHeaders describe the message: content type, cache rules, cookies, accepted encodings, authentication, security policies, and more. Headers made HTTP extensible.","The body carries content\nThe body may contain HTML, JSON, CSS, JavaScript, images, video chunks, or uploaded form data. Not every request or response needs a body.","Sources: RFC 9110; MDN Evolution of HTTP"],["Core Concepts","IT 3203 • Research and Learn Web Evolution • 06","HTTP message parts and why they matter","Understanding the parts of an HTTP message explains how web apps communicate.","Start line\nThe request start line contains the method, path, and protocol version. The response start line contains the HTTP version, status code, and reason phrase. This first line sets the basic purpose of the message.","Headers\nHeaders are key-value pairs. They can declare the media type, compression, cache policy, language, credentials, cookies, browser identity, cross-origin rules, and security settings.","Blank line\nA blank line separates metadata from the message body. Even though that looks small, clear boundaries are necessary so a server or browser can parse the message reliably.","Body\nThe body is the actual payload. For a web page response it may be HTML. For an API response it is commonly JSON. For uploads it may include form fields or file data.","Statelessness\nHTTP is stateless, which means each request should be understandable by itself. Web sites create “memory” across visits by using cookies, sessions, tokens, caches, and server-side databases.","Version independence\nModern HTTP separates semantics from wire format. The meaning of a method, status code, or header can stay the same even as HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 change how messages are encoded.","Sources: RFC 9110"],["History","IT 3203 • Research and Learn Web Evolution • 07","Origin story: the web needed four building blocks","HTTP was invented as one part of the original World Wide Web system.","1989","Berners-Lee proposes hypertext system at CERN","1990","WorldWideWeb browser and server are implemented","1991","First servers outside CERN and public announcement","1996","HTTP/1.0 documented in RFC 1945","1997","HTTP/1.1 standardized in RFC 2068","2015","HTTP/2 published after SPDY work","2022","HTTP core, HTTP/2, HTTP/3 updated in RFC 911x","Four original web building blocks\nMDN describes the original Web as including HTML for documents, HTTP to exchange documents, a browser to display and edit documents, and an HTTP server to provide access. HTTP’s job was not to create the web page; it was to move the web resource.","Key historical lesson\nHTTP succeeded because it was simple enough for early browsers and servers, but flexible enough to evolve. Later versions added metadata, caching, security, and performance improvements while keeping the same basic idea.","Sources: W3C original WWW proposal; MDN Evolution of HTTP"],["History","IT 3203 • Research and Learn Web Evolution • 08","HTTP/0.9: the one-line protocol","The first HTTP design was tiny because the early web mostly transferred simple HTML documents.","What a request looked like\nGET /index.html","What came back\nOnly the HTML file itself. There were no status codes, no headers, no content type, and no formal error handling built into the protocol.","Only GET was available, so the protocol was built for retrieving documents rather than submitting complex data.\n\nThe request did not include the full URL because the client was already connected to the target server.\n\nThe response did not identify the type of file, so the early model worked best for HTML and text documents.\n\nErrors had to be explained inside returned HTML instead of through standardized status codes like 404 or 500.\n\nHTTP/0.9 shows the original purpose clearly: ask for a web page and receive the page content.","Why it could not last\nOnce the web needed images, forms, different media types, and automatic browser behavior, HTTP needed metadata. That pushed the protocol toward HTTP/1.0 and headers.","Sources: MDN Evolution of HTTP"],["History","IT 3203 • Research and Learn Web Evolution • 09","HTTP/1.0: headers made HTTP extensible","HTTP/1.0 turned a simple file request into a richer message format.","Major additions\nVersion number in the request\nStatus code line in the response\nHeaders for metadata\nContent-Type for non-HTML files","Why status codes mattered\nA browser could now recognize success or failure without reading a human-facing error page. This allowed automatic redirects, caching decisions, retries, and better error screens.","Why Content-Type mattered\nThe web could carry images, style sheets, scripts, downloads, and later JSON APIs because the response could label the kind of content being sent.","HTTP/1.0 also reveals a challenge of fast growth: features were added by browsers and servers before strong standardization. MDN notes that from 1991–1995, many changes were adopted through a “try-and-see” approach. That made the web innovative but created interoperability problems when clients and servers interpreted behavior differently.","Research insight\nExtensibility is one reason HTTP survived. New headers can be created for caching, privacy, CORS, CSP, cookies, authentication, and more without redesigning the whole protocol.","Sources: MDN Evolution of HTTP; RFC 1945"],["History","IT 3203 • Research and Learn Web Evolution • 10","HTTP/1.1: standardization and connection reuse","HTTP/1.1 became the long-lasting foundation of the modern web.","Problem before HTTP/1.1\nA page with HTML, images, CSS, and scripts could require many separate connections. Each new TCP connection added setup time and slowed page loading.","HTTP/1.1 solution\nPersistent connections allowed one TCP connection to be reused for multiple requests and responses. This reduced repeated connection setup.","Other improvements\nHTTP/1.1 added or clarified pipelining, chunked transfer, stronger caching, content negotiation, and the Host header.","Host header and virtual hosting\nThe Host header allowed multiple domains to share one IP address. That mattered as the public web grew because hosting every site on a unique IP address was not practical.","Why HTTP/1.1 lasted\nHTTP/1.1 was stable, widely supported, and flexible. Many tools, servers, proxies, and teaching examples still use HTTP/1.1 because it is readable and remains a useful fallback.","MDN notes that HTTP/1.1 was first published in January 1997 and remained stable for more than 15 years before HTTP/2. Its durability came from the ability to improve behavior without breaking the core request/response model.","Sources: MDN Evolution of HTTP; RFC 2068; RFC 9112"],["History","IT 3203 • Research and Learn Web Evolution • 11","HTTP/1.1 changed web hosting and caching","HTTP evolved from “download a page” to “coordinate efficient web delivery.”","Caching\nCache-Control, ETag, Last-Modified, and related headers help browsers and proxies decide whether they can reuse a resource instead of downloading it again. Good caching saves bandwidth and makes pages faster.","Content negotiation\nA client can advertise what formats, languages, or encodings it accepts. A server can choose an appropriate response, such as compressed content or a preferred language.","Compression\nHeaders such as Accept-Encoding and Content-Encoding allow compressed responses. This lowers bytes transferred, which improves performance on slow or mobile networks.","Intermediaries\nHTTP is not only browser-to-origin-server. Proxies, caches, gateways, load balancers, and CDNs can sit between client and server. This made HTTP a building block for internet-scale delivery.","Lesson\nHTTP became powerful because it could describe how data should be handled, not just carry the data. That is why headers are as important as the body of the message.","Sources: RFC 9110; RFC 9111; MDN Evolution of HTTP"],["Problem","IT 3203 • Research and Learn Web Evolution • 12","The performance problem: latency and head-of-line blocking","As pages became larger, HTTP/1.1’s connection model became a bottleneck.","Web pages became heavier\nModern pages include HTML, CSS, JavaScript bundles, fonts, images, analytics, ads, videos, API calls, and third-party scripts. A single page may need many HTTP requests.","Browsers opened parallel connections\nTo work around waiting, browsers commonly used several TCP connections per origin. That improved speed but increased connection overhead and reduced network efficiency.","Head-of-line blocking\nWhen responses share an ordered path, one slow or blocked item can delay items behind it. HTTP/1.1 pipelining had this issue, and HTTP/2 later solved only part 